<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vindhya Range</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vindhya Range is a broken chain of hills and plateau scarps across west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, running roughly east to west north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> valley. It has traditionally been seen as the boundary between northern India and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. Its hills and forests bordered regions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelkhand">Bundelkhand</a>, and many small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> lay among them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vindhya-range.jpg" alt="Vindhya Range" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Varun Shiv Kapur from New Delhi, India · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_valley_between_Malwa_and_Mandu_plateaus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
